NNAAMMEE
       tlsproxy - Postfix TLS proxy

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ttllsspprrooxxyy [generic Postfix daemon options]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  ttllsspprrooxxyy(8)  server  implements a two-way TLS proxy. It is used by
       the ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8) server to talk SMTP-over-TLS with remote SMTP clients
       that are not allowlisted (including clients whose allowlist status  has
       expired),  and  by  the ssmmttpp(8) client to support TLS connection reuse,
       but it should also work for non-SMTP protocols.

       Although one ttllsspprrooxxyy(8) process can serve  multiple  sessions  at  the
       same  time,  it  is a good idea to allow the number of processes to in‐
       crease with load, so that the service remains responsive.

PPRROOTTOOCCOOLL EEXXAAMMPPLLEE
       The example below  concerns  ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8).  However,  the  ttllsspprrooxxyy(8)
       server is agnostic of the application protocol, and the example is eas‐
       ily adapted to other applications.

       After  receiving a valid remote SMTP client STARTTLS command, the ppoosstt‐‐
       ssccrreeeenn(8) server sends the remote SMTP client endpoint string, the  re‐
       quested role (server), and the requested timeout to ttllsspprrooxxyy(8).  ppoosstt‐‐
       ssccrreeeenn(8)  then receives a "TLS available" indication from ttllsspprrooxxyy(8).
       If the TLS service is available, ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8) sends  the  remote  SMTP
       client  file  descriptor  to  ttllsspprrooxxyy(8),  and sends the plaintext 220
       greeting to the remote SMTP client.  This triggers TLS negotiations be‐
       tween the remote SMTP client and ttllsspprrooxxyy(8).  Upon completion  of  the
       TLS-level  handshake,  ttllsspprrooxxyy(8) translates between plaintext from/to
       ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8) and ciphertext to/from the remote SMTP client.

SSEECCUURRIITTYY
       The ttllsspprrooxxyy(8) server is moderately security-sensitive.  It  talks  to
       untrusted  clients  on  the network. The process can be run chrooted at
       fixed low privilege.

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
       Problems and transactions are logged to ssyyssllooggdd(8) or ppoossttllooggdd(8).

